1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
Leave seafood out too long, and it smells

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:03,240
fishy.

3
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:06,320
Much of that smell comes from trimethylamine, an

4
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:06,880
amine.

5
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:09,160
Squeeze some lemon juice over it, and the

6
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,320
smell fades.

7
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,680
The amine's solution turns red litmus blue, so

8
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:14,880
it's a base.

9
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:18,480
Ethanamide has a nitrogen too, but its solution

10
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:20,280
leaves litmus unchanged.

11
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,120
So why is one nitrogen compound a base,

12
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,880
while the other barely is?

13
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:30,260
An amine is like ammonia, N H three,

14
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:32,660
with one or more hydrogens swapped for carbon

15
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:33,780
chains.

16
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:36,260
The nitrogen keeps its lone pair, a pair

17
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:38,980
of electrons that isn't used in bonding.

18
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:41,780
To classify an amine, count the carbon groups

19
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:43,060
on the nitrogen.

20
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:46,340
One is primary, two is secondary, and three

21
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:47,700
is tertiary.

22
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:49,940
That's not the alcohol rule.

23
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:53,060
In propan-two-amine, the N H two sits on

24
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:55,620
a middle carbon, but the nitrogen has only

25
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:58,740
one carbon group, so it's a primary amine.

26
00:01:00,767 --> 00:01:03,646
To name a primary amine, drop the alkane's

27
00:01:03,727 --> 00:01:06,606
final e, and add amine, with a number

28
00:01:06,687 --> 00:01:10,286
for the nitrogen's carbon when it's needed: ethanamine,

29
00:01:10,367 --> 00:01:13,446
propan-one-amine and propan-two-amine.

30
00:01:13,807 --> 00:01:16,126
If the nitrogen carries more than one carbon

31
00:01:16,207 --> 00:01:18,766
group, the longest chain is the parent, and

32
00:01:18,847 --> 00:01:20,846
the other groups go in front, with an

33
00:01:20,927 --> 00:01:22,606
N to show they're on the nitrogen.

34
00:01:23,247 --> 00:01:29,246
That gives N-methylethanamine, and with two methyls, N,N-dimethylethanamine.

35
00:01:31,267 --> 00:01:33,746
An amide has a C double bond O

36
00:01:33,827 --> 00:01:36,546
bonded straight to the nitrogen, C O N

37
00:01:36,707 --> 00:01:37,826
H two.

38
00:01:37,907 --> 00:01:40,306
It's like an ester, with nitrogen in place

39
00:01:40,347 --> 00:01:42,066
of the single bonded oxygen.

40
00:01:42,387 --> 00:01:44,626
It forms when a carboxylic acid and an

41
00:01:44,707 --> 00:01:47,346
amine are heated strongly together.

42
00:01:47,427 --> 00:01:51,026
They join and lose water, a condensation reaction,

43
00:01:51,147 --> 00:01:52,546
like esterification.

44
00:01:52,867 --> 00:01:55,106
To name an amide, drop the e and

45
00:01:55,187 --> 00:01:56,626
add amide.

46
00:01:56,707 --> 00:01:58,866
The C double bond O carbon is carbon

47
00:01:59,027 --> 00:02:03,266
one, so there's no number: ethanamide, propanamide.

48
00:02:05,267 --> 00:02:06,546
Now the forces.

49
00:02:06,787 --> 00:02:09,826
A primary amine has two N H bonds,

50
00:02:09,907 --> 00:02:12,946
so it can hydrogen bond, like an alcohol.

51
00:02:13,027 --> 00:02:16,626
But nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen, so

52
00:02:16,707 --> 00:02:19,186
an N H bond is less polar, and

53
00:02:19,267 --> 00:02:21,666
its hydrogen bonds are weaker.

54
00:02:21,787 --> 00:02:25,666
Propan-one-amine and propan-one-ol have the same number of

55
00:02:25,747 --> 00:02:28,786
electrons, yet the amine boils at forty eight

56
00:02:28,867 --> 00:02:31,586
degrees, and the alcohol at ninety seven.

57
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:36,480
The number of N H hydrogens matters too.

58
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:39,760
These three amines are isomers, so their dispersion

59
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:41,600
forces are roughly similar.

60
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:45,600
Propan-one-amine has two N H hydrogens.

61
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:49,200
N-methylethanamine has only one, so it forms fewer

62
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:51,840
hydrogen bonds, and boils at about thirty seven

63
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:53,120
degrees.

64
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:56,880
Trimethylamine has none, so its molecules can't hydrogen

65
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:58,840
bond to each other, and it boils at

66
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:00,800
about three degrees.

67
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:03,760
Small amines still mix with water, because water

68
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:06,560
hydrogen bonds to the nitrogen's lone pair.

69
00:03:08,567 --> 00:03:11,286
Amides go the other way, even though N

70
00:03:11,367 --> 00:03:13,726
H is less polar than O H.

71
00:03:14,007 --> 00:03:17,046
Propanamide and propanoic acid have the same number

72
00:03:17,087 --> 00:03:19,846
of electrons, but the amide boils at about

73
00:03:19,927 --> 00:03:22,166
two hundred and thirteen degrees.

74
00:03:22,247 --> 00:03:24,726
The difference is how many hydrogen bonds each

75
00:03:24,807 --> 00:03:26,406
molecule makes.

76
00:03:26,527 --> 00:03:29,366
Each primary amide molecule has two N H

77
00:03:29,447 --> 00:03:31,926
hydrogens to give, and its C double bond

78
00:03:32,007 --> 00:03:34,726
O oxygen takes hydrogen bonds.

79
00:03:34,887 --> 00:03:37,686
So each molecule links to several neighbours, building

80
00:03:37,767 --> 00:03:41,046
a network, while acid molecules mostly pair off

81
00:03:41,167 --> 00:03:42,086
as dimers.

82
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:45,500
Back to the litmus.

83
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:48,500
In water, the amine's lone pair takes a

84
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:51,460
proton from a water molecule, like ammonia in

85
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:52,580
Module six.

86
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:55,620
That forms an ethylammonium ion, and leaves a

87
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:57,860
hydroxide ion behind.

88
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:01,380
The raised hydroxide ion concentration turns red litmus

89
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:02,580
blue.

90
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:05,620
But the reaction's reversible, and the equilibrium lies

91
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:08,340
well to the left, so most amine molecules

92
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:09,940
stay unchanged.

93
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:11,940
Amines are weak bases.

94
00:04:13,967 --> 00:04:17,406
With a stronger proton donor, like hydrochloric acid,

95
00:04:17,487 --> 00:04:19,166
the reaction goes to completion.

96
00:04:19,647 --> 00:04:23,566
Ethanamine and H C l form ethylammonium chloride,

97
00:04:23,607 --> 00:04:24,606
an ionic salt.

98
00:04:25,087 --> 00:04:27,566
Its ions attract each other strongly, so the

99
00:04:27,647 --> 00:04:29,486
salt barely evaporates.

100
00:04:29,727 --> 00:04:30,926
That's the lemon trick.

101
00:04:31,487 --> 00:04:34,206
Citric acid in the juice gives trimethylamine a

102
00:04:34,287 --> 00:04:36,846
proton, turning much of it into a salt

103
00:04:36,967 --> 00:04:38,286
that stays on the fish.

104
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:42,820
So why isn't ethanamide a base?

105
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:45,140
Its nitrogen has a lone pair too.

106
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,420
But the C double bond O beside it

107
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:50,220
pulls that lone pair in, and shares it

108
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:52,900
across the nitrogen, carbon and oxygen.

109
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:54,620
It's the same effect you saw in the

110
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:58,060
carboxylate ion: sharing loosens the grip on a

111
00:04:58,140 --> 00:04:58,940
proton.

112
00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:02,020
That helped ethanoic acid let its proton go.

113
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:05,900
Here, ethanamide barely takes a proton from water,

114
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,220
so its solution is close to neutral.

115
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:09,900
It isn't an acid either.

116
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:11,980
It has no O H to give away,

117
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:13,660
so litmus doesn't change.

118
00:05:15,667 --> 00:05:19,106
Now, organic acid against organic base.

119
00:05:19,187 --> 00:05:21,906
A carboxylic acid gives a proton, from its

120
00:05:21,987 --> 00:05:23,026
O H.

121
00:05:23,147 --> 00:05:25,586
An amine takes a proton, with its lone

122
00:05:25,667 --> 00:05:26,466
pair.

123
00:05:26,547 --> 00:05:28,066
Both are weak.

124
00:05:28,147 --> 00:05:31,586
The acid raises the hydronium ion concentration, so

125
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:33,666
blue litmus turns red.

126
00:05:33,747 --> 00:05:37,266
The amine raises the hydroxide ion concentration, so

127
00:05:37,347 --> 00:05:39,226
red litmus turns blue.

128
00:05:39,587 --> 00:05:41,586
Mix the two at room temperature, and the

129
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:44,786
proton jumps across, making a salt, such as

130
00:05:44,867 --> 00:05:46,626
